From 94368544c117404bf75d72f94c9a176019280d2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 20:16:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] mbox: instrument the property channel (nextbsd-kernel#171)

Diagnostic only, no behaviour change beyond logging. Never merge.

Enabling bcm2835_firmware.c on BCM2712 (#171) makes it claim /soc/firmware,
which generic simple_mfd claimed before -- it wins the probe with
BUS_PROBE_DEFAULT against simple_mfd's BUS_PROBE_GENERIC. That regresses the
HDMI framebuffer console: fbd's attach now fails with

    fb0: <BCM2835 VT framebuffer driver> on simplebus0
    mbox0: mbox response error
    device_attach: fb0 attach returned 6

The two boots are otherwise identical line-for-line up to that point; the
only structural difference is simple_mfd1 vs bcm2835_firmware0 on the
firmware node. Both drivers attach at the same pass
(BUS_PASS_BUS + BUS_PASS_ORDER_LATE), so this is not an ordering problem,
and the only functional difference between their attach paths is that
bcm2835_firmware calls OF_device_register_xref() and simple_mfd does not.

What makes this worth instrumenting rather than bisecting: the same mailbox
works. dev.bcm2835_firmware.0.temperature returns 35.6C at runtime on the
very kernel where fbd failed at boot, through this identical function. So
one kernel gives a within-kernel A/B -- a failing call and a succeeding one
-- and the answer is in what differs between them.

Three probes:

  req   the tag, the buffer's bus address, and the value actually written to
        the mailbox register. Those last two are the point: the address is
        truncated with (uint32_t)msg_phys, so a buffer busdma placed above
        4GB is silently sent to the wrong place and the firmware parses
        whatever happens to be at the truncated address. This board has 16GB.
        bcm283x_dmabus_peripheral_lowaddr() -- the constraint meant to
        prevent exactly that -- is printed alongside so it can be seen to be
        applied.

  rsp   the response channel readback and the VideoCore's response code,
        spelled out as SUCCESS / NOT success.

  err   the existing "mbox response error" now prints the code it rejected.
        It never did, which is why the original failure said nothing useful.

Reading it, comparing fbd's GET_PHYSICAL_W_H (0x00040003) at boot against
GET_TEMPERATURE (0x00030006) from the sysctl later:

  phys differs, one above 4GB   the DMA path -- the buffer is being placed
                                where the 32-bit register cannot address it,
                                and NextBSD's out-of-tree busdma patch
                                (0016, D58105) is implicated. That would have
                                consequences well beyond this board.
  phys identical                firmware-side state: claiming the node
                                changes what the VideoCore will answer, and
                                the xref registration is the thing to test
                                next.

Refs nextbsd-kernel#171, and #111 -- the framebuffer console is what makes
milestone 1 functional while KMS does not exist, so a latent way to break it
from an unrelated driver is worth understanding rather than working around.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01By8iBEpeLNRmud8GTFiSyc
---
 sys/arm/broadcom/bcm2835/bcm2835_mbox.c | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/arm/broadcom/bcm2835/bcm2835_mbox.c b/sys/arm/broadcom/bcm2835/bcm2835_mbox.c
index a3a3744ce43..736afc41833 100644
--- a/sys/arm/broadcom/bcm2835/bcm2835_mbox.c
+++ b/sys/arm/broadcom/bcm2835/bcm2835_mbox.c
@@ -350,7 +350,8 @@ bcm2835_mbox_err(device_t dev, bus_addr_t msg_phys, uint32_t resp_phys,
 		return (EIO);
 	}
 	if (msg->code != BCM2835_MBOX_CODE_RESP_SUCCESS) {
-		device_printf(dev, "mbox response error\n");
+		device_printf(dev, "mbox response error (code 0x%08x)\n",
+		    msg->code);
 		return (EIO);
 	}
 
@@ -418,6 +419,39 @@ bcm2835_mbox_property(void *msg, size_t msg_size)
 
 	memcpy(buf, msg, msg_size);
 
+	/*
+	 * [MBOX-DIAG] nextbsd-kernel#171: which property calls succeed, and at
+	 * what address.
+	 *
+	 * The interesting column is phys vs wrote. The address handed to the
+	 * VideoCore is truncated to 32 bits below -- (uint32_t)msg_phys -- so a
+	 * buffer that busdma placed above 4GB is silently sent to the wrong
+	 * place and the firmware answers on garbage. bcm283x_dmabus_peripheral_
+	 * lowaddr() is the constraint that is supposed to prevent that, so it is
+	 * printed alongside.
+	 *
+	 * On the failing kernel, fbd's GET_PHYSICAL_W_H (tag 0x00040003) fails
+	 * during attach while GET_TEMPERATURE (0x00030006) succeeds later from a
+	 * sysctl, through this identical path. Comparing the two lines is the
+	 * whole point: same phys => firmware-side state, different phys => the
+	 * DMA path, and NextBSD's out-of-tree busdma patch is implicated.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct bcm2835_mbox_tag_hdr *dtag;
+		uint32_t dtag0 = 0;
+
+		if (msg_size >= sizeof(struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr) +
+		    sizeof(struct bcm2835_mbox_tag_hdr)) {
+			dtag = (struct bcm2835_mbox_tag_hdr *)((uint8_t *)msg +
+			    sizeof(struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr));
+			dtag0 = dtag->tag;
+		}
+		device_printf(mbox, "[MBOX-DIAG] req tag=0x%08x len=%zu "
+		    "phys=0x%016jx wrote=0x%08x lowaddr=0x%016jx\n",
+		    dtag0, msg_size, (uintmax_t)msg_phys, (uint32_t)msg_phys,
+		    (uintmax_t)bcm283x_dmabus_peripheral_lowaddr());
+	}
+
 	bus_dmamap_sync(msg_tag, msg_map,
 	    BUS_DMASYNC_PREWRITE);
 
@@ -429,6 +463,12 @@ bcm2835_mbox_property(void *msg, size_t msg_size)
 
 	memcpy(msg, buf, msg_size);
 
+	/* [MBOX-DIAG] what the VideoCore actually said. */
+	device_printf(mbox, "[MBOX-DIAG] rsp chan=0x%08x code=0x%08x%s\n",
+	    reg, ((struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr *)msg)->code,
+	    ((struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr *)msg)->code ==
+	    BCM2835_MBOX_CODE_RESP_SUCCESS ? " (SUCCESS)" : " (NOT success)");
+
 	err = bcm2835_mbox_err(mbox, msg_phys, reg,
 	    (struct bcm2835_mbox_hdr *)msg, msg_size);
 
-- 
